// four_bar — a four-bar linkage: a ground link of length ground_len, a crank, a coupler and
// a rocker. Crank pivot at the origin of the instance, rocker pivot at (ground_len, 0).
//
//   import "../lib/four_bar.mech"
//   use four_bar(crank_len = 0.1) as lk on bench at (0, 0)
//   drive lk.crank at 36 deg/s from 60 deg
//
// Ports:
//   crank, coupler, rocker      the three moving rods (A and B ends as usual)
// The defaults are a crank-rocker (Grashof: shortest + longest < the other two). With the crank
// at angle theta and the rocker at angle phi (both measured from the ground link; the rocker from
// its A end, the ground pivot, up to B), the closed loop gives Freudenstein's equation
//   cos(theta - phi) = K3 + (d/a) cos(phi) - (d/c) cos(theta),
//   K3 = (a^2 - b^2 + c^2 + d^2) / (2 a c)
// with d = ground_len, a = crank_len, b = coupler_len, c = rocker_len.
part four_bar(ground_len = 0.4, crank_len = 0.1, coupler_len = 0.35, rocker_len = 0.3) {
  ground frame {
    anchor p1 at (0, 0)
    anchor p2 at (ground_len, 0)
  }

  rod crank   { length: crank_len }
  rod coupler { length: coupler_len }
  rod rocker  { length: rocker_len }

  pin crank.A   -> frame.p1
  pin coupler.A -> crank.B
  pin rocker.A  -> frame.p2
  pin coupler.B -> rocker.B
}
